A. Importing Libraries
- Pandas is used for loading, cleaning, transforming, merging, and analyzing data.
- Matplotlib is used to display box plots.

B. Loading the Dataset
- read_csv() loads the real estate dataset.
- The raw string r before the file path helps Python interpret backslashes correctly.
- head() displays the first five records.

C. Cleaning Column Names
- df.columns accesses all column names.
- str.strip() removes spaces at the beginning and end.
- str.lower() converts names to lowercase.
- str.replace(" ", "_") replaces spaces with underscores.
- For example, "Sale Price" becomes "sale_price".

D. Handling Missing Values and Duplicates
- isnull().sum().sum() counts all missing cells in the DataFrame.
- drop_duplicates() removes duplicate rows.
- dropna(subset=["price"]) removes rows where the price is missing.
- median(numeric_only=True) calculates medians for numerical columns only.
- fillna() fills remaining missing values in numerical columns with their medians.

E. Converting Date
- str[:8] selects the first eight characters from each date string.
- pd.to_datetime() converts the selected date text into datetime values.
- The resulting date column supports operations such as extracting the year.
- This expression assumes the source date values are strings in a compatible format.

F. Merging ZIP-Code Average Price
- groupby("zipcode") groups records by ZIP code.
- mean() calculates the average price in each ZIP-code group.
- rename("zip_avg_price") gives the resulting Series a meaningful name.
- merge() joins this average-price information back to the original DataFrame using zipcode as the common key.

G. Filtering Records
- dt.year extracts the year from the date column.
- between(3, 4) selects properties with 3 or 4 bedrooms.
- The == operator selects ZIP code 98103.
- The & operator combines all three conditions; each condition is enclosed in parentheses.
- len(subset) displays the number of matching records.

H. Creating Property Categories
- pd.cut() divides numerical bedroom counts into intervals.
- The bins [-1, 2, 4, 100] define the category ranges.
- labels assign the names Small, Medium, and Large.
- get_dummies() converts the property_type categories into numerical indicator columns.
- df_encoded stores the encoded DataFrame.

I. Aggregation
- groupby("property_type") groups properties by category.
- mean() calculates average price for each property category.
- groupby("zipcode") calculates average price for each ZIP code.
- sort_values(ascending=False) sorts averages from highest to lowest.
- head() displays the first five results.

J. Outlier Detection
- quantile([0.25, 0.75]) calculates Q1 and Q3.
- IQR is Q3 minus Q1.
- The lower and upper limits are Q1 - 1.5 × IQR and Q3 + 1.5 × IQR.
- boxplot() displays the price distribution before and after filtering.
- between() retains prices inside the calculated limits.

K. Saving the Dataset
- to_csv() exports the cleaned data.
- index=False prevents the DataFrame index from being added as a separate column.

